<section style="padding-top:40px">
  <div class="wrap" style="max-width:900px">

    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lead">We scored 32 badminton club-night products identified in our market search against a rubric written before any of them was looked at, weighted for a UK social club night: 18 to 26 players, four courts, mixed strength, people arriving late and leaving early. <strong>Then we ran the identical evaluation three times, through three different AI models, to test whether the result depended on who was reading.</strong> First place held. The scores - and much of the ordering below it - did not, and that turns out to be the most useful thing on this page.</p>

      <p><strong>What this covers, and what it does not.</strong> These are products for <strong>running the club night</strong> - who plays next, on which court, with whom. If what your committee actually needs is membership records, subscriptions or court booking, that is a different category of <a href="/badminton-club-management-software">badminton club management software</a>, and the products that do it well are named below: ShuttleOps and ShuttleStats for subs and payments, ServeLeague and SportyHQ for membership and leagues. ePegboard does not collect money or run a booking system.</p>

      <p style="color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.95rem"><strong>Disclosure:</strong> we make ePegboard, one of the products here. We wrote the rubric and ran the evaluations; nobody independent commissioned them. Every prompt and every unedited response is published, including the run that scores us lowest, and every product below links to the page its evaluation started from - its own site, or its app listing where that is all it publishes - so you can check us.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="guide-toc" style="margin-top:28px">
      <h4>In this comparison</h4>
      <a href="#glance">At a glance: the best option for each requirement</a>
      <a href="#how">How we compared them</a>
      <a href="#shortlist">The shortlist, compared in detail</a>
      <a href="#runs">The three evaluation results</a>
      <a href="#holds">What held across all three</a>
      <a href="#table">All 32 products and their scores</a>
      <a href="#weak">Where ePegboard is weak</a>
      <a href="#others">When another product is the better answer</a>
      <a href="#limits">Limits of this comparison</a>
      <a href="#practice">Whether clubs actually stay</a>
    </div>

    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:34px">

      <h2 id="glance">At a glance: the best option for each requirement</h2>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Best overall for a mixed-strength club night: <a href="https://www.epegboard.com">ePegboard</a>.</strong> First in all three evaluations. Free for clubs.</li>
        <li><strong>Best when the hall has no reliable connection: <a href="https://shuttlsync.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ShuttlSync</a> or <a href="https://racketsocial.app" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Racket Social</a>.</strong> Both run a session offline at no cost. We cannot.</li>
        <li><strong>Best run one-handed from a phone: <a href="https://shuttlepilot.io" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ShuttlePilot</a>.</strong> Built for an organiser who is also holding a racket.</li>
        <li><strong>Best for collecting session fees: <a href="https://shuttlestats.app" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ShuttleStats</a>.</strong> The club keeps its own Stripe account and pays a flat fee per payment.</li>
        <li><strong>Best for leagues and a member register: <a href="https://serveleague.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ServeLeague</a>.</strong> Six documented league formats, with membership categories and voting rights.</li>
        <li><strong>Best as a straight peg-board replacement: <a href="https://www.court-manager.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Court Manager</a>.</strong> The most thoroughly documented picker configuration in the field.</li>
        <li><strong>Best if you would rather print the evening: <a href="https://sportrevive.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SportRevive</a>.</strong> The whole night generated in advance as one balanced sheet.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="how">How we compared them</h2>

      <div class="guide-callout">
        <h4>Fifteen criteria, one hundred points, written down before anything was looked at</h4>
        <p style="margin-top:8px"><strong>Five of them are the club night itself and carry 48 of the 100 points:</strong> arrivals and late joiners, choosing the next game as courts free up, balancing the four, sharing out sit-outs, and varying who plays with whom.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:10px">The other ten cover explaining a pick, manual override, ratings, live scoring and history, the court display, attendance reporting, payments, competitions, resilience and price.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><strong>A product only takes full marks where it describes how the thing behaves</strong> - the rule, the edge cases, the limits. A feature bullet scores half.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:10px">Where we could find no evidence for a capability at all, it contributes <strong>zero to the evidenced score</strong> - but we also publish an upper bound that assumes every unresolved capability is present. <strong>Missing documentation therefore creates uncertainty about a product rather than proof that it cannot do something</strong>, which is why every figure below is a range.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:12px"><a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/compare/method">Read the full rubric and the evidence rules</a></p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="shortlist">The shortlist, compared in detail</h2>

      <p>Ten products a UK club could realistically choose between, with what each is best at and the thing most likely to rule it out. The other 22 are in the full table below.</p>

      <div class="cmp-wrap">
        <table class="cmp-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Product</th><th>Best for</th><th>What stands out</th><th>Main limitation</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            @foreach (var s in shortlist)
            {
              <tr>
                <td><a href="@s.Url" target="_blank" rel="noopener">@s.Product</a></td>
                <td>@s.BestFor</td>
                <td>@s.StandsOut</td>
                <td>@s.Limit</td>
              </tr>
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 id="runs">The three evaluation results</h2>

      <p>Same fifteen criteria, same weights, same club profile, same candidate list, same day. The only thing that changed between them was the model doing the reading.</p>

      <div class="cmp-wrap">
        <table class="cmp-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Model</th><th>Retrieval</th><th>Field</th><th>ePegboard</th><th>Second place</th><th>Margin</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            @foreach (var r in runs)
            {
              <tr>
                <td>@r.Model</td><td>@r.Retrieval</td><td>@r.Field</td>
                <td><strong>@r.Us</strong></td><td>@r.Second</td><td>@r.Margin</td>
              </tr>
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 id="holds">What held across all three</h2>

      <div class="guide-callout">
        <h4>The ranking was far more stable than the scores</h4>
        <p style="margin-top:8px"><strong>ePegboard ranked first in all three evaluations, but its absolute score ranged from 70.5 to 95.5</strong> - from the same inputs, on the same day, with nothing changed but which model was reading.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:10px">That is why we treat individual scores cautiously and put weight only on findings that repeat across all three runs. Anyone quoting a single number off a comparison like this, ours included, is quoting the evaluator rather than the product.</p>
      </div>

      <p><strong>Second place is not stable either.</strong> ShuttlSync takes it in two runs; ShuttleFlow and Racket Social tie for it in the third, where ShuttlSync drops to eighth. In the full table you will find products that move forty points between runs.</p>

      <p><strong>Our lead over second place was 6.0, 8.5 and 10.0 points</strong> - always positive, never comfortable, and smallest in the run that read the field most completely. Six points on a hundred-point rubric is not a verdict. It is a reason to put two or three products side by side on a real club night.</p>

      <h2 id="table">All 32 products and their scores</h2>

      <p><strong>Every figure is a range:</strong> the lower bound is what that run could evidence, the upper bound is what the product would reach if every criterion it could not establish turned out to be fully met. A single figure means nothing was left unresolved. A dash means that run resolved nothing for that product - <strong>never zero, and never a judgement on it.</strong></p>

      <div class="cmp-wrap">
        <table class="cmp-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Product</th><th>Claude Opus 5</th><th>ChatGPT (free)</th><th>ChatGPT 5.6</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            @foreach (var r in rows)
            {
              <tr>
                @* Every product links to the URL its evaluation started from - the disclosure
                   above promises exactly this, and until 2026-09-12 only the shortlist kept it. *@
                <td>
                  @if (r.Product == "ePegboard")
                  {
                    <a href="@r.Url" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>ePegboard</strong></a>
                  }
                  else
                  {
                    <a href="@r.Url" target="_blank" rel="noopener">@r.Product</a>
                  }
                </td>
                <td>@r.Opus</td>
                <td>@r.Free</td>
                <td>@r.Sol</td>
              </tr>
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p style="margin-top:18px"><strong>Read across the rows, not down the columns.</strong> ShuttleFlow is 55.0 in one run and 85.5 in another. Racket Social 77.5, 85.5 and 50.0. The disagreement is the honest finding, and any comparison claiming a confident ordering of thirty-two products is selling you something.</p>

      <p><strong>Court Buddy deserves a note.</strong> It scores well and its sit-out documentation is among the clearest in the field, but its website is a pickleball session manager and never mentions badminton, though its app listing does name badminton club nights.</p>

      <p><strong>We also gave each evaluation a 33rd candidate that does not exist.</strong> We invented a product name, gave it a plausible address and listed it among the real ones as a hallucination control. <strong>No run scored it</strong> - all three reported it unlocatable and moved on. It is not counted in the 32 above, and it is not a competitor.</p>

      <div class="guide-callout">
        <h4>If we have got something wrong about your product</h4>
        <p style="margin-top:8px">This page makes scored, factual claims about 32 named products, and we will have made mistakes. We made four in our first attempt and corrected them publicly, including telling readers that a real competitor did not exist.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><strong>If you make one of these products and something here is wrong, tell us and point us at the evidence.</strong> A link to the page that shows it is enough. We will correct factual errors quickly, re-run the rubric against a URL you choose if the scoring is what is wrong, and publish the outcome either way - including where it moves a competitor above us.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:10px">We would rather be corrected by you than leave something inaccurate on a page about your business.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:12px"><a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/contact">Tell us what we got wrong</a></p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="weak">Where ePegboard is weak</h2>

      <p>Taken from the evaluations' own assessments of the leader:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>We do not have a current answer for offline.</strong> The web app needs a connection. Running fully offline means our separate Windows desktop product, and that product is no longer in development - major bug fixes only. If your hall genuinely cannot hold a connection, we are not the right choice today.</li>
        <li><strong>Payments are a record, not a till.</strong> We log what was collected. Cash, cheque or transfer, entered afterwards, one attendee at a time. Your treasurer still does the collecting.</li>
        <li><strong>We never write down what happens when somebody turns up late.</strong> They check in, join the queue, and their waiting time counts from that moment - but we do not say so anywhere. ShuttlePilot and vsBadminton both spell their handling out; we should spell ours out too.</li>
        <li><strong>Competitions are summarised, not documented</strong>, and are handicap doubles only - singles and team formats are not built.</li>
        <li><strong>We optimise for a tablet or laptop.</strong> It runs on a phone perfectly well, but a bigger screen gives the better experience. ShuttlePilot goes further and is designed for one-handed phone use.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="others">When another product is the better answer</h2>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>A hall with no reliable connection: ShuttlSync or Racket Social.</strong> Both do real offline operation at no cost - roster prepared online, session run offline, synced after. Our only offline option is a paid Windows product we are no longer developing.</li>
        <li><strong>Collecting the money rather than recording it: ShuttleStats or ShuttleOps.</strong> ShuttleStats puts the club on its own Stripe account, charges per payment with no subscription, and handles capacity, waiting lists and refunds.</li>
        <li><strong>Running the whole night one-handed from a phone: ShuttlePilot.</strong> Built around the fact that the person running club night is usually also holding a racket.</li>
        <li><strong>Leagues and a membership register: ServeLeague or SportyHQ.</strong> Six documented league formats, with membership categories and voting rights, against our handicap doubles.</li>
        <li><strong>Printing the whole evening in advance: SportRevive.</strong> A different model to live picking, and for a stable group of nine it is less fuss than any queue.</li>
        <li><strong>A rating players take seriously: UBR or Ladderly.</strong> Both publish their maths in more depth than we publish ours.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="limits">Limits of this comparison</h2>

      <p><strong>The rubric cannot see what a vendor does not write down.</strong> A product that does something well but never documents it scores as though it does not do it. Several products here publish a landing page, and the honest reading of a low score is <em>unknown</em>, not <em>bad</em>. That is why every figure is a range.</p>

      <p><strong>Nothing was demonstrated.</strong> Of thirty-two scored products, exactly one had any capability watched working rather than described - and only because it publishes nothing to describe, so the evaluator ran it instead. Every other score here, ours first, is a vendor's account of its own product. Ours describes what ePegboard actually does, and the demo is there so you can check that in about a minute rather than take it on trust.</p>

      <div class="guide-callout">
        <h4>We got the first version of this wrong</h4>
        <p style="margin-top:8px">Our original evaluation missed three real competitors, failed to render six JavaScript sites and read another product at the wrong address - then recorded that product as publishing nothing. We corrected the candidate set and the retrieval method and reran all three evaluations from scratch. Nothing from the original attempt is reported here.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:10px">The full account of what went wrong, and what each error cost, is on the <a href="/compare/method">method page</a>.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="practice">Whether clubs actually stay</h2>

      <p>A documentation score measures what a product says about itself. The question underneath it is whether clubs run a second night:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>More than nine in ten</strong> clubs that ran a real first session with ePegboard ran another - 23 of the 25 that have had the chance.</li>
        <li><strong>Seven in ten</strong> have run five or more - 11 of 16. Every club whose first night came before the summer break came back.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Measured on 12 September 2026, across clubs whose first session was in 2026, excluding demo, template and our own test clubs. A "session" means one where a game was actually started. <strong>A club counts only once it has had the chance</strong> - two weeks of term time for the second night, six for the fifth - because a club that started last week cannot have come back yet, and the school holidays are not chances either. <a href="/compare/method#retention">The full working is on the method page</a>, including the two figures we published before this one and why both were wrong.</p>

      <div class="guide-callout" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h4>The test that beats all three of these</h4>
        <p style="margin-top:8px">Run your own awkward night: a late arrival after three games, someone sitting out and rejoining, two courts finishing together, a pairing you want kept, a suggestion you overrule, and a wrong score that needs correcting. Then ask the organiser whether it took decisions out of their head or just moved them onto a screen.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:14px">ePegboard is free for clubs, and so are several of the products above. Try more than one.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:14px"><a class="btn btn-primary" href="https://play.epegboard.com/instant-demo" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Try the instant demo</a> &nbsp; <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="https://play.epegboard.com/signup" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Set up a free club</a></p>
      </div>

